debug 2026-06-05T03:36:06.045+000The logs you provided show the successful initialization of the RocksDB metadata store for Ceph Monitor B (mon.b). The storage layer initialized without syntax or disk space faults, and the monmap correctly detected the other cluster monitors (mon.noname-a, mon.noname-c).However, since this log cuts off right at the execution loop, the actual problem is a split-brain scenario or network isolation (lack of Mon Quorum).The Underlying Issue: Missing QuorumNotice this line in your monitor map:0: [v2:10.233.15.190:3300/0... mon.noname-a2: [v2:10.233.52.0:3300/0... mon.noname-bmon.b is identifying itself on IP 10.233.52.0.In your previous operator logs, Ceph health timed out trying to reach 10.233.15.190 (mon.a) and 10.233.52.0 (mon.b).Because these monitor pods are isolated on different overlay network subnets (Calico/Flannel inside your VM cluster), they cannot talk to each other to establish a majority quorum. Without a quorum, the cluster enters an active freeze, preventing the OSD daemon processes from mapping /dev/vdb or /dev/vdc.Step-by-Step Recovery Action1. Confirm the Monitor Pod StatesCheck whether all three monitor pods are fully running, or if they are crashlooping due to network timeouts:bashkubectl get pods -n rook-ceph -l app=rook-ceph-mon -o wide
Use code with caution.Look closely at the IPs. Ensure the VMs hosting these pods have security groups/firewalls allowing traffic on ports 6789 and 3300.2. Test Inter-Pod Network ConnectivityPick one of your running mon pods and attempt to ping/nc the IP of another monitor to see if your virtual machine network is blocking cross-node traffic:bashkubectl exec -n rook-ceph -it <name-of-mon-b-pod> -- nc -zv 10.233.15.190 3300
Use code with caution.If this times out, your Kubernetes CNI network provider cannot route storage control traffic between your VM cluster nodes.3. Update to Host Networking (Highly Recommended for VMs)If you are running Rook-Ceph inside Virtual Machines, standard Kubernetes overlay networking often suffers from MTU or encapsulation drops that kill Ceph Mon quorums. The industry standard fix is to bypass the CNI entirely for storage control traffic.Edit your CephCluster CRD file:yamlspec:
  network:
    provider: host
Use code with caution.Applying this config maps the Ceph daemons directly to the real IP addresses of your VMs, eliminating the virtual network routing errors.4. The Last Resort: Mon Failover TriggerIf the network is fine but the state is corrupted from a split-brain deployment, force Rook to elect a new leader by reducing the expected monitor count down to 1 temporarily in your cluster.yaml spec:yamlspec:
  mon:
    count: 1
    allowMultiplePerNode: true
Use code with caution.Apply this change, wait for the operator to clean up the stale monitor states, and then scale it back up to 3 once the single leader stabilizes.What CNI (e.g., Calico, Cilium, Flannel) are you using on your control node, and did the nc network probe connect successfully?
